      "writtenOn": "2026-10-01T12:34:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "`checkOSNRaz` and `checkOSNRza` populate the impairments builder differently. The A-to-Z version sets all three accumulated values (lines 681–683):\n\n```\n.setAccumulatedCd(Uint32.valueOf(Math.round(signal.get(\"calcCd\"))))\n.setAccumulatedPmd2(Uint32.valueOf(Math.round(signal.get(\"calcPmd2\"))))\n.setAccumulatedPdl2(Uint32.valueOf(Math.round(signal.get(\"calcPdl2\"))))\n```\n\nThe Z-to-A version leaves out `setAccumulatedPdl2(...)` (lines 914–915). calcPdl2 is still calculated and logged in the ZA case, but it never reaches ZToAImpairmentsBuilder. \n\nPlease add the missing setter to checkOSNRza:\n\n```\n.setAccumulatedPdl2(Uint32.valueOf(Math.round(signal.get(\"calcPdl2\"))))\n```\n\nThe two methods are now nearly identical up to this point. A small helper that fills the shared impairment-params fields would prevent this kind of drift. A unit test that checks every field on both builders for the same path would also catch it.",
      "revId": "cab96d9e0c3658414de25a7747d48d76bce7023f",
      "serverId": "7fc14799-209e-464c-9743-7a06c2c21a81"
